Hi all,

 I had to reinstall INLA on the linux cluster at my work and I keep getting this error in using Rgeneric

Error in exists("my.cache", envir = envir, mode = "list") :
  use of NULL environment is defunct

The code I had been using before is below, and it comes from the line

envir = environment(sys.call()[[1]])

but, I am unsure what to do to fix it.

Thank you,

Jessica Godwin

rw.new <http://rw.new> = function(cmd = c("graph", "Q", "mu", "initial", "log.norm.const", "log.prior", "quit"), theta = NULL){
  ## assume 'tau', 'order', 'n' and 'm' 'n' is the dim of RW and 'm' is the aggregated length,
  ## averaging over n/m variables, non-overlapping

  ## the environment of this function which holds the variables and we can store 'my.cache'
  ## there.
  envir = environment(sys.call()[[1]])

  if (!exists("my.cache", envir = envir, mode = "list")) {
    nn = n %/% m
    stopifnot (nn == as.integer(n/m))
    R = INLA:::inla.rw <http://inla.rw>(n, order = order,  scale.model=TRUE, sparse=TRUE)
    A = matrix(0, nn, n)
    j = 1
    for(i in 1:nn) {
      A[i, j:(j+m-1)] = 1/m
      j = j + m
    }
    A = inla.as.sparse(A)
    D = Diagonal(nn, x=1)
    assign("my.cache", list(R=R, A=A, D=D, nn=nn), envir = envir)
  }

  interpret.theta = function() {
    return(list(kappa = exp(theta[1L])))
  }

  graph = function() {
    return (Q())
  }

  Q = function() {
    QQ = rBind(cBind(p$kappa * my.cache$R + tau * t(my.cache$A) %*% my.cache$A,
                     -tau * t(my.cache$A)),
               cBind(-tau * my.cache$A, tau * my.cache$D))
    return(QQ)
  }

  mu = function() {
    return(numeric(0))
  }

  log.norm.const = function() {
    val = (n-order) * (-0.5 * log(2 * pi) + 0.5 * log(p$kappa)) +
      (my.cache$nn * (-0.5 * log(2 * pi) + 0.5 * log(tau)))
    return(val)
  }

  log.prior = function() {
    val = dgamma(p$kappa, shape = shape0, rate = rate0, log = TRUE) + theta[1]
    return(val)
  }

  initial = function() {
    return(4)
  }

  quit = function() {
    return(invisible())
  }

  ## as some calls to this function does not define 'theta',  its convenient to have to
  ## defined still (like in the graph-function)
  if (is.null(theta))
    theta = initial()

  p = interpret.theta()
  val = do.call(match.arg(cmd), args = list())
  return(val)
}

Dear all,

I thought I had a simple problem, but somehow I cannot answer this myself... Instead of having a structure (say "rw1") over 1 variable, I want it over multiple variables. To make it concrete, say I have a continuous Y variable, and three CONTINUOUS X variables. The measurement Eq. is straightforward;
Y = b1X1 + b2X2 + b3X3 + e,

with e being gaussian errors. Again, this is simple enough and can be estimated by INLA or even OLS. However, I want to have the following state Eq;
b1 ~ N(0,5);   # or whatever, has to be largely un-infomative. Where 5 is the std.dev <http://std.dev>.

for(k = 2:3){    # in reality I have about 10 of these...
   b_{k} ~ b_{k-1} + n;
}

with n ~ N(0,5); # or whatever again.

So I hope you see my issue here, and hopefully I simply missed a simple solution here. I don't have reproducible code, given that I haven't even attempted this yet. (I do know how to do it in Stan/JAGS for example, so I want to know if I can do this first...)
Many thanks, as always,
Alex

